ps me steal the show.  \\nFrom [CITY_NAME] streets to the [DESTINATION],  \\nThis phone\\u2019s got power for every situation.  \\n\\nNo lag, no limits, just smooth communication\\u2014  \\n[PRICE] puts innovation in circulation.  \\nSo raise it up high when the beat drops true:  \\nThe future fits right in the palm of you.  \\n\\nP.P.S Don\\u2019t forget to charge it before the encore.\", \"checks\": {\"0:detectable_content:postscript\": true, \"1:detectable_content:number_placeholders\": true}}", "evidence_refs": ["seed-202/failure/round-1/diagnostic/ifeval-1619"], "partition": "train", "schema_version": 1}, "outcome": "success", "schema_version": 1}, {"case_id": "sha256:f02167476f590e7c907dd541eebd8e9b229f2b844949d8ebee6d685a896f2908", "record": {"task_id": "ifeval-2853", "family": "ifeval-source-2853", "sample_id": "sha256:2b28565e45d4fbde07ddeac6db9b4cce567bc5d0a1269876e66d55203b5a3171", "attempt": 0, "policy_id": "sha256:53801304990b98a1f9f7e0aa33b487fa1b0170eed9d91002ab99029fa5016997", "environment_id": "ifeval-strict-e6890f85757dd84e27ca6df2dd30651dafad28e0", "status": "completed", "passed": true, "complete": true, "instruction": "Write a poem about flooding in Donnell, TX. The poem should have a title in double angular brackets, i.e. <<title>>, and contains at least 3 words in all capital letters.", "feedback": "{\"response\": \"<<Donnell After the Rain>>\\n\\nThe river climbed the fences in the night,  \\nand swallowed roads beneath a sheet of gray;  \\nDONNELL woke to thunder without light,  \\nwhile fields became a moving inland bay.  \\n\\nThe church bell rang\\u2014STAY HIGH, STAY CALM, STAY NEAR\\u2014  \\nas porches floated softly past the square;  \\nyet neighbors shared their boats, their bread, their cheer,  \\nand lifted hope above the rising air.  \\n\\nBy dawn, the sun broke gold across the flood,  \\non rooftops shining like a promise new;  \\nfrom WATER, mud, and memory arose  \\na town that stood\\u2014and helped each other through.\", \"checks\": {\"0:change_case:capital_word_frequency\": true, \"1:detectable_format:title\": true}}", "evidence_refs": ["seed-202/failure/round-1/diagnostic/ifeval-2853"], "partition": "train", "schema_version": 1}, "outcome": "success", "schema_version": 1}, {"case_id": "sha256:f488acec9886a9d8c7d570ddcb9c2b1d31f5a073f114e23d117c6e85d44a4b73", "record": {"task_id": "ifeval-3518", "family": "ifeval-source-3518", "sample_id": "sha256:c0df25974691be3509aaff924b96e8490a0ae09f22a3d16d7ef33142eb067a3b", "attempt": 0, "policy_id": "sha256:53801304990b98a1f9f7e0aa33b487fa1b0170eed9d91002ab99029fa5016997", "environment_id": "ifeval-strict-e6890f85757dd84e27ca6df2dd30651dafad28e0", "status": "completed", "passed": true, "complete": true, "instruction": "Could you please give me the pros and cons of working abroad wrapped in JSON format. Please make sure that your response only contains a JSON block. Please also make sure to include keywords 'compensated' and 'immigrants' in the response.", "feedback": "{\"response\": \"{\\n  \\\"pros\\\": [\\n    \\\"Higher earning potential and better compensated opportunities\\\",\\n    \\\"International work experience and career growth\\\",\\n    \\\"Exposure to new cultures, languages, and perspectives\\\",\\n    \\\"Opportunities to build a global professional network\\\",\\n    \\\"Improved independence and adaptability\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"cons\\\": [\\n    \\\"Homesickness and separation from family or friends\\\",\\n    \\\"Cultural and language barriers\\\",\\n    \\\"Visa, tax, and legal complexities\\\",\\n    \\\"Potential discrimination or challenges faced by immigrants\\\",\\n    \\\"Higher relocation costs and unfamiliar living conditions\\\"\\n  ]\\n}\", \"checks\": {\"0:detectable_format:json_format\": true, \"1:keywords:existence\": true}}", "evidence_refs": ["seed-202/failure/round-1/diagnostic/ifeval-3518"], "partition": "train", "schema_version": 1}, "outcome": "success", "schema_version": 1}, {"case_id": "sha256:f7d9c7c0f981aeccd6289f95535fceef7282a721add8cb51efa5a6d0ea2fa0e8", "record": {"task_id": "ifeval-2785", "family": "ifeval-source-2785", "sample_id": "sha256:55d4a8cc3b77ffe3dd273caeaa76965faad7ce3d35aa307f2841b96924abf972", "attempt": 0, "policy_id": "sha256:53801304990b98a1f9f7e0aa33b487fa1b0170eed9d91002ab99029fa5016997", "environment_id": "ifeval-strict-e6890f85757dd84e27ca6df2dd30651dafad28e0", "status": "completed", "passed": true, "complete": true, "instruction": "What is inside Shinto shrines? Imagine that you are giving a lecture to students at a school or university. Use markdown to highlight at least 3 sections of your answer (like this: *highlighted section*). Your answer must also contain at least 3 placeholders (an example of a placeholder is [address]).", "feedback": "{\"response\": \"## *What You May Find Inside a Shinto Shrine*\\n\\nGood morning, everyone. A Shinto shrine, or **jinja**, is a sacred place where kami\\u2014spirits or divine presences\\u2014are honored. Unlike many temples or churches, shrines are not usually designed for large indoor congregations.\\n\\n### *1. The Main Sanctuary*\\n\\nAt the heart of the shrine is the **honden**, or main sanctuary. It contains the **shintai**, a sacred object believed to embody or represent the kami. Visitors generally cannot enter this building; it is usually closed and approached from outside.\\n\\nThe shintai may be:\\n\\n- A mirror\\n- A sword\\n- A sacred jewel\\n- A natural object, such as a stone or tree\\n\\nThe actual object is often hidden from public view.\\n\\n### *2. Worship Areas*\\n\\nVisitors commonly pray at the **haiden**, or worship hall. A typical visit involves:\\n\\n1. Washing at a **temizuya** purification fountain\\n2. Approaching the offering box\\n3. Bowing, making an offering, and clapping twice\\n4. Offering a silent prayer\\n5. Bowing once more\\n\\nAt some shrines, priests perform ceremonies inside these areas for weddings, festivals, or special prayers.\\n\\n### *3. Sacred Objects and Offerings*\\n\\nShrines may contain or display:\\n\\n- **Ofuda**, paper or wooden talismans for protection\\n- **Omamori**, small protective amulets\\n- **Ema**, wooden prayer plaques\\n- Ritual tools used by priests\\n- Offerings such as rice, sake, fruit, and salt\\n\\nVisitors may purchase an amulet for [purpose] or write a prayer on an ema using the name [name].\\n\\n### *4. Other Shrine Buildings*\\n\\nA larger shrine complex may also include:\\n\\n- A **torii** gate marking the entrance to sacred space\\n- A **shamusho**, or shrine office\\n- Residences or preparation rooms for priests\\n- Storehouses\\n- A stage for ritual dances or performances\\n- Smaller subsidiary shrines dedicated to other kami\\n\\n### *Conclusion*\\n\\nThe most important thing inside a Shinto shrine is not decoration or public gathering space, but the sacred presence of the kami. Much of the shrine\\u2019s holiness is expressed through boundaries, purification, ritual objects, and the careful separation between ordinary human space and sacred space. Visitors should follow local instructions and observe customs at [shrine location].\", \"checks\": {\"0:detectable_format:number_highlighted_sections\": true, \"1:detectable_content:number_placeholders\": true}}", "evidence_refs": ["seed-202/failure/round-1/diagnostic/ifeval-2785"], "partition": "train", "schema_version": 1}, "outcome": "success", "schema_version": 1}], "memory": []}

